.TH NETMAPPER 1 "2026-09-30" "netmapper 1.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
netmapper \- independent bounded TCP and UDP network scanner
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B netmapper
.B \-t
.I target
[\fB\-t\fR \fItarget\fR ...]
[\fB\-p\fR \fIports\fR | \fB\-\-top-ports\fR \fIn\fR | \fB\-\-all-ports\fR]
[\fB\-\-scan-mode\fR \fItcp|udp|both\fR]
[\fB\-s\fR]
[\fB\-\-format\fR \fItable|json\fR]
[\fB\-o\fR \fIfile\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B netmapper
is an independent network scanner. It resolves IPv4/IPv6 addresses, CIDR networks, and DNS names, then uses bounded TCP connect probes and UDP request/response probes. It can collect limited HTTP or passive TCP banner evidence and emit a terminal table or JSON report.
.PP
Netmapper does not invoke or require Nmap. It does not currently implement raw-packet scan types, OS fingerprinting, comprehensive service/version fingerprints, traceroute, or an extensible script engine. It does not claim full Nmap feature parity.
.PP
Only scan systems and networks that you own or are explicitly authorized to assess.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t , \ \-\-target \ target
Target IP address, CIDR network, or DNS name. Repeat for multiple targets.
.TP
.BR \-p , \ \-\-ports \ ports
Comma-separated ports and inclusive ranges, for example 22,53,80,443,8000-8100. Port zero is invalid.
.TP
.BR \-\-top-ports \ n
Select the first 1 to 100 entries from the built-in common-port set. Default: 100.
.TP
.BR \-\-all-ports
Scan ports 1-65535 for selected protocol(s). Scans are limited to one million probes per run.
.TP
.BR \-\-scan-mode \ mode
Select tcp (default), udp, or both.
.TP
.BR \-c , \ \-\-concurrency \ n
Maximum simultaneous probes per protocol, from 1 to 8192. Default: 512.
.TP
.BR \-\-timeout-ms \ milliseconds
Per-probe timeout, from 50 to 60000 milliseconds. Default: 1000.
.TP
.BR \-s , \ \-\-service-detection
Collect an HTTP response header or a passive TCP banner from open TCP ports. This is not comprehensive service fingerprinting.
.TP
.BR \ \-\-format \ format
Output as table (default) or JSON. Interactive table output highlights port numbers in orange.
.TP
.BR \-o , \ \-\-output \ file
Write formatted results to a file instead of standard output.
.TP
.BR \-h , \ \-\-help
Print command help.
.TP
.BR \-V , \ \-\-version
Print version.
.SH STATES
.TP
.B open
The TCP connection completed or a UDP response was received.
.TP
.B closed
The remote endpoint explicitly refused the TCP connection.
.TP
.B filtered
The attempt timed out or failed without an explicit connection refusal.
.TP
.B open|filtered
A UDP probe received no response before timeout. The port may be open or filtered.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Scan default common TCP ports on an authorized target:
.B netmapper -t 192.0.2.15
.TP
Scan both transports on selected ports:
.B netmapper -t 192.0.2.15 --scan-mode both -p 22,53,80,443
.TP
Scan all TCP ports:
.B netmapper -t 192.0.2.15 --all-ports
.TP
Collect TCP banner evidence and write a JSON report:
.B netmapper -t 192.0.2.15 -p 22,80,443 -s --format json -o scan.json
.SH LIMITATIONS
The built-in scanner is a bounded inventory scanner. It does not currently match all Nmap features. TCP connect probes can complete connections on open ports. UDP silence is ambiguous; the built-in DNS and NTP probes are limited, and other UDP ports receive an empty datagram. Results depend on routing, firewalls, packet loss, and remote rate limiting. Scanning may trigger monitoring or network policy controls.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ip (8)
